II are correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dba1"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dba2", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["617aa9e8ef3fe65ad8e836b3"], "concepts": ["61ab78a66fe44bfe0fe4a345"], "question": {"text": "Which one of the following statements for D.I. Mendeleeff, is incorrect ?"}, "options": [{"id": "697c5b", "text": "He authored the textbook - Principles of Chemistry.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dba3"}, {"id": "f2d4b6", "text": "At the time, he proposed Periodic Table of elements structure of atom was known.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dba4"}, {"id": "2f10c1", "text": "Element with atomic number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>101</mn></math> is named after him.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dba5"}, {"id": "416037", "text": "He invented accurate barometer.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dba6"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dba7", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e567ac499d1e49588f95ec"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95cc"], "question": {"text": "The IUPAC symbol for the element with atomic number <math><mn>119</mn></math> would be:"}, "options": [{"id": "96d4e9", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dba8"}, {"id": "166e79", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dba9"}, {"id": "d3b06a", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbaa"}, {"id": "63b1fd", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbab"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbac", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477e"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52dc"], "question": {"text": "The third ionization enthalpy is minimum for:"}, "options": [{"id": "1b1e74", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbad"}, {"id": "0a725b", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbae"}, {"id": "c1178f", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbaf"}, {"id": "52bee3", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbb0"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbb1", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477e"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e56"], "question": {"text": "Which one of the following elements will remain as liquid inside pure boiling water?"}, "options": [{"id": "d79316", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ga</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbb2"}, {"id": "cc4052", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Br</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbb3"}, {"id": "2d70a7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Li</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbb4"}, {"id": "f84c19", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cs</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbb5"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbb6", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e567ac499d1e49588f95ec"], "concepts": ["6162fb7e6ed13040ec84f909"], "question": {"text": "Identify the correct statements about p-block elements and their compounds.\n(A) Non metals have higher electronegativity than metals.\n(B) Non metals have lower ionisation enthalpy than metals.\n(C) Compounds formed between highly reactive nonmetals and highly reactive metals are generally ionic.\n(D) The non-metal oxides are generally basic in nature.\n(E) The metal oxides are generally acidic or neutral in nature.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "871b78", "text": "(B) and (D) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbb7"}, {"id": "56ab66", "text": "(A) and (C) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbb8"}, {"id": "697b0f", "text": "(D) and (E) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbb9"}, {"id": "211982", "text": "(B) and (E) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbba"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbbb", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["617aa9e8ef3fe65ad8e836b3"], "concepts": ["6117b89067dbe2603c3e80cb"], "question": {"text": "Match the LIST-I with LIST-II.<br />$\\begin{array}{|l|l|l|l|}\\hline & \\begin{array}{c} \\text{LIST-I} \\\\ \\text{(Family)} \\end{array} & & \\begin{array}{c} \\text{LIST-II} \\\\ \\text{(Symbol of Element)} \\end{array} \\\\ \\hline \\text{A.} & \\begin{array}{l} \\text{Pnicogen} \\\\ \\text{(group 15)} \\end{array} & \\text{I.} & \\text{Ts} \\\\ \\hline \\text{B.} & \\text{Chalcogen} & \\text{II.} & \\text{Og} \\\\ \\hline \\text{C.} & \\text{Halogen} & \\text{III.} & \\text{Lv} \\\\ \\hline \\text{D.} & \\text{Noble gas} & \\text{IV.} & \\text{Mc} \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}$<br />Choose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "2ce2ed", "text": "A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbbc"}, {"id": "dc03f1", "text": "A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbbd"}, {"id": "5fe6e8", "text": "A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbbe"}, {"id": "5d3578", "text": "A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbbf"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbc0", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e567ac499d1e49588f95ec"], "concepts": ["6162fb7e6ed13040ec84f90d"], "question": {"text": "The group number, number of valence electrons, and valency of an element with atomic number <math><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo></math> respectively, are:"}, "options": [{"id": "89cb61", "text": "<math><mn>15,6</mn></math> and <math><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbc1"}, {"id": "a62ea6", "text": "<math><mn>16,6</mn></math> and <math><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbc2"}, {"id": "4b38ae", "text": "<math><mn>16,5</mn></math> and <math><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbc3"}, {"id": "33e5d4", "text": "<math><mn>15,5</mn></math> and <math><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbc4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbc5", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477e"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f926d"], "question": {"text": "For electron gain enthalpies of the elements denoted as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>eg</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math>, the incorrect option is :"}, "options": [{"id": "556237", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>eg</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>Cl</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>eg</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbc6"}, {"id": "c4187b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>eg</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>Se</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>eg</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbc7"}, {"id": "50b4a3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>eg</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>eg</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>At</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbc8"}, {"id": "7b8284", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>eg</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>Te</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>eg</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>Po</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbc9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbca", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["617aa9e8ef3fe65ad8e836b3"], "concepts": ["61ab78a66fe44bfe0fe4a345"], "question": {"text": "Given below are two statements :\n<strong> Statement (I):</strong> According to the Law of Octaves, the elements were arranged in the increasing order of their atomic number.<br><strong> Statement (II):</strong>  Meyer observed a periodically repeated pattern upon plotting physical properties of certain elements against their respective atomic numbers.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "9b62c4", "text": "Both <strong> Statement (I)</strong> and<strong> Statement (II)</strong> are false.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbcb"}, {"id": "464f20", "text": "Both <strong> Statement (I)</strong> and<strong> Statement (II)</strong> are true.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbcc"}, {"id": "8ce460", "text": "<strong> Statement (I)</strong> is false but<strong> Statement (II)</strong> is true.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbcd"}, {"id": "c2aac6", "text": "<strong> Statement (I)</strong> is true but<strong> Statement (II)</strong> is false.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbce"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbcf", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477e"], "concepts": ["60e567c3499d1e49588f994c"], "question": {"text": "<p>In which of the following pairs, electron gain enthalpies of constituent elements are nearly the same or identical?</p><p>(A) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Rb</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cs</mi></math></p><p>(B) Na and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p>(C) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ar</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Kr</mi></math></p><p>(D) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>At</mi></math></p><p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "1a6a03", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>&nbsp;only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbd0"}, {"id": "ce064f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbd1"}, {"id": "56c813", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbd2"}, {"id": "554cdc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math>&nbsp;only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbd3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbd4", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e567ac499d1e49588f95ec"], "concepts": ["657569de99fd55951d1a47d9"], "question": {"text": "Which of the following is a metalloid?"}, "options": [{"id": "2111b3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Sc</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbd5"}, {"id": "4138f2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Pb</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbd6"}, {"id": "482fc6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Bi</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbd7"}, {"id": "1f43ab", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Te</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbd8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbd9", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477e"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4734"], "question": {"text": "The correct option with respect to the Pauling electronegativity values of the elements is:"}, "options": [{"id": "e8ef85", "text": "$\\mathrm{Te}>\\mathrm{Se}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbda"}, {"id": "32054b", "text": "$\\mathrm{Ga}  < \\mathrm{Ge}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbdb"}, {"id": "66af14", "text": "$\\mathrm{Si}  < \\mathrm{Al}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbdc"}, {"id": "2b74af", "text": "$\\mathrm{P}>\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbdd"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbde", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e567ac499d1e49588f95ec"], "concepts": ["6162fb7e6ed13040ec84f911"], "question": {"text": "The element that does not belong to the same period of the remaining elements (modern periodic table) is:"}, "options": [{"id": "09c62b", "text": "Iridium", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbdf"}, {"id": "4e9d39", "text": "Platinum", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbe0"}, {"id": "dd2e08", "text": "Osmium", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbe1"}, {"id": "a3c500", "text": "Palladium", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbe2"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbe3", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477e"], "concepts": ["60e567c3499d1e49588f9940"], "question": {"text": "Number of elements from the following that CANNOT form compounds with valencies which match with their respective group valencies is ______.\nB, C, N, S, O, F, P, Al, Si"}, "options": [{"id": "2ac327", "text": "7", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbe4"}, {"id": "51657a", "text": "3", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbe5"}, {"id": "9be530", "text": "5", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbe6"}, {"id": "bfc5f1", "text": "6", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbe7"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbe8", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e567ac499d1e49588f95ec"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95cc"], "question": {"text": "The atomic number of the element unnilennium is :"}, "options": [{"id": "71be80", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>109</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbe9"}, {"id": "d4d184", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>102</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbea"}, {"id": "858d82", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>108</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbeb"}, {"id": "8d9cee", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>119</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbec"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbed", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477e"], "concepts": ["60e567dd499d1e49588f9cbb"], "question": {"text": "For compound having the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>GaAlCl</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>, the correct option from the following is"}, "options": [{"id": "aefade", "text": "Ga is coordinated with CI in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>GaAlCl</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbee"}, {"id": "967268", "text": "Ga is more electronegative than AI and is present as a cationic part of the salt <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>GaAlCl</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbef"}, {"id": "7fcfcc", "text": "CI forms bond with both AI and Ga in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>GaAlCl</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbf0"}, {"id": "056b67", "text": "Oxidation state of Ga in the salt <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>GaAlCl</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>is</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbf1"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbf2", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e567ac499d1e49588f95ec"], "concepts": ["6162fb7e6ed13040ec84f90d"], "question": {"text": "The elements with atomic numbers <math><mrow><mn>101</mn></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mn>104</mn></mrow></math> belong to, respectively:"}, "options": [{"id": "e2974e", "text": "Group <math><mrow><mn>11</mn></mrow></math> and Group <math><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbf3"}, {"id": "954d07", "text": "Actinoids and Group <math><mn>6</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbf4"}, {"id": "6db5b7", "text": "Actinoids and Group <math><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbf5"}, {"id": "cfa2ee", "text": "Group <math><mn>6</mn></math> and Actinoids", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbf6"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbf7", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477e"], "concepts": ["617aa9e7ef3fe65ad8e83669"], "question": {"text": "Aluminium is usually found in <math><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math> oxidation state. In contrast, thallium exists in <math><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math> and <math><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math> oxidation states. This is due to:"}, "options": [{"id": "96fb14", "text": "Diagonal relationship", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbf8"}, {"id": "085c4d", "text": "Lattice effect", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbf9"}, {"id": "3bd650", "text": "Lanthanoid contraction", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbfa"}, {"id": "72ef3b", "text": "Inert pair effect", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbfb"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbfc", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e567ac499d1e49588f95ec"], "concepts": ["657569de99fd55951d1a47d9"], "question": {"text": "The metal that has very low melting point and its periodic position is closer to a metalloid is"}, "options": [{"id": "37036a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbfd"}, {"id": "2b2ef7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ga</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbfe"}, {"id": "7a7a23", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Se</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dbff"}, {"id": "a8e7a0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>In</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dc00"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dc01", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d3035eaf049203d85366a4"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477e"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b41"], "question": {"text": "<p>The ionic radii of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math> respectively are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>33</mn></math>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>, while the covalent radius of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>74</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&nbsp;</p><p>The correct statement for the ionic radius of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math> from the following is :</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "71635c", "text": "It is smaller than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dc02"}, {"id": "187f4c", "text": "It is bigger than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dc03"}, {"id": "6aaeff", "text": "It is bigger than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>, but smaller than of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dc04"}, {"id": "d59383", "text": "It is smaller than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>, but bigger than of<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f692ee0e063d927dc05"}], "quest